  "worked_solution": "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,\u21d4\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(C-5I) & =3-\\text{rank}(C-5I) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(C-5I)={0}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C-5I)=0,$ we obtain that $C-5I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=C-5I$ is non-singular, then $(C-5I)x=0$ has only zero solution.  As a result, $\\lambda =5$ can not be an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
